<body>
    <h1>Order Orchestration Process</h1>
    <div class=3D"Section1">
        <p><a class=3D"external-link" href=3D"https://docs.google.com/sprea=
dsheets/d/1qR-XArODMjNtecdPj3_VG5QGGwlzthQkWkmIRZmp6vo/edit?gid=3D0#gid=3D0=
" rel=3D"nofollow"><u>Order Orchestration Process Document</u></a></p>
<p></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Due 100% completed by 11/14</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>We expect that 80% will be straightforward / less complex. Due 11/7</p><=
/li>
<li>
<p>We expect that 20% will be more complex. Due 11/14</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p>Review by 11/24</p></li>
</ul>
<h2 id=3D"OrderOrchestrationProcess-Homework">Homework</h2>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p>Identify all of the unique <strong>Fulfillment Processes </strong>that n=
eed to exist.</p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><strong>Name </strong>each unique Fulfillment Process.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Which <strong>Scenarios (Actions) </strong>are the processes applicable =
to? (Choose one or many of the below for a given Fulfillment Process).</p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p>New (Add)</p></li>
<li>
<p>Amend</p></li>
<li>
<p>Renew</p></li>
<li>
<p>Cancel</p></li>
</ol></li>
<li>
<p>What <strong>Steps (Tasks)</strong> need to be completed for a given pro=
duct to be fulfilled? Note: A collection of <strong>Steps (Tasks)</strong> =
forms a <strong>Fulfillment Process</strong>.</p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p>Step (Task) <strong>Name</strong>: Give the Task a Name</p></li>
<li>
<p>Step (Task) <strong>Type</strong>: Is the Task <strong>Manual </strong>o=
r <strong>Automated</strong>?</p></li>
<li>
<p>Step (Task) <strong>Owner</strong>:<strong> </strong>Which <strong>Perso=
na </strong>owns this Step (Task)?</p></li>
<li>
<p>Step (Task) <strong>Assignment Method</strong>: How do we <strong>assign=
 </strong>a Step (Task) to a Persona? (see definitions below)&nbsp;</p></li=
>
<li>
<p>Step (Task) <strong>Execution Trigger</strong>: When should the Step (Ta=
sk) begin?<strong> </strong>(see definitions below)</p></li>
<li>
<p>Step (Task) <strong>SLA</strong>: <strong>&nbsp;</strong>(if any)</p></l=
i>
<li>
<p><strong>Point of No Return</strong>: The point after which we must creat=
e an Amendment (vs updating the existing Order) (see definitions below)</p>=
</li>
<li>
<p>Step (Task) <strong>Dependencies</strong>:<strong> </strong>(if any)</p>=
</li>
</ol></li>
<li>
<p>Identify <strong>Swimlanes </strong>(Task Group) for the defined Steps (=
Tasks) for a given Fulfillment Process. Note:<strong> </strong>There can be=
 as many or as few Swimlanes as needed to stay organized.</p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><strong>Swimlane </strong>Sequence: Define in what order the Swimlanes s=
hould be executed.&nbsp;</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Task </strong>Sequence: Define in what order the Steps (Tasks) s=
hould be executed within each Swimlane</p></li>
</ol></li>
</ol></li>
<li>
<p>For each <strong>Product</strong>, identify the<strong> Fulfillment Proc=
ess</strong> that needs to occur.&nbsp;</p></li>
</ol>
<p></p>
<p></p>
<h1 id=3D"OrderOrchestrationProcess-Definitions">Definitions</h1>
<p></p>
<h2 id=3D"OrderOrchestrationProcess-Steps(Tasks/FulfillmentSteps)DefiningSp=
ecificActivities"><strong>Steps (Tasks / Fulfillment Steps)</strong> Defini=
ng Specific Activities</h2>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Auto Task</strong> - An automated system activity that executes =
without human intervention (e.g., =E2=80=9CGenerate License Key,=E2=80=9D =
=E2=80=9CCreate Tenant,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9CUpdate CRM Record=E2=80=9D). Acti=
ons complete automatically when triggered.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Manual Task</strong> - A human-performed activity requiring some=
one to complete work and mark it done (e.g., =E2=80=9CShip Hardware,=E2=80=
=9D =E2=80=9CConfigure Network Settings,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9CConduct Training=
=E2=80=9D). These create tasks assigned via your chosen assignment method.<=
/p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Milestone</strong> - A checkpoint marking significant progress t=
hat can trigger revenue recognition events (e.g., =E2=80=9CGo-Live Confirme=
d,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9CDelivery Completed=E2=80=9D). Critical for linking ful=
fillment to revenue.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Pause</strong> - A planned pause in the process (e.g., =E2=80=9C=
Wait for Payment Clearance,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9CHold Until Contract Start Dat=
e=E2=80=9D). Ensures proper timing and sequencing.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Callout</strong> - An integration step that calls external syste=
ms or APIs (e.g., =E2=80=9CProvision in Cloud Platform,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9CV=
alidate with Compliance Service,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9CGenerate in License Serv=
er=E2=80=9D). Enables real-time system integration.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Staged Assetize</strong> - Converts fulfillment data into Asset =
records for ongoing lifecycle management (e.g., =E2=80=9CCreate Subscriptio=
n Asset,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9CRegister Hardware Asset=E2=80=9D). Critical for =
renewals and amendments.</p></li>
</ul>
<p></p>
<h2 id=3D"OrderOrchestrationProcess-TaskAssignmentMethods"><strong>Task Ass=
ignment Methods</strong></h2>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><strong>Context Based</strong></p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p>Assigns based on the context of the fulfillment order/obligation</p></li=
>
<li>
<p>Uses attributes from the order, account, or product to determine assignm=
ent</p></li>
<li>
<p>Most flexible and intelligent option</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p>Account Owner: Assigns to whoever owns the customer account</p></li>
<li>
<p>Product Specialist: Routes based on product family</p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p>Cyber products =E2=86=92 Cyber fulfillment team</p></li>
<li>
<p>Tech products =E2=86=92 Tech fulfillment team</p></li>
</ol></li>
<li>
<p>&nbsp;Geographic: Routes based on customer location&nbsp;</p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p>US customers =E2=86=92 US fulfillment team</p></li>
<li>
<p>EU customers =E2=86=92 EU fulfillment team</p></li>
</ol></li>
<li>
<p>Contract Value: High-value orders =E2=86=92 Senior specialists</p></li>
</ol></li>
</ol></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Least Loaded</strong></p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p>Assigns to the team member with the fewest active assignments</p></li>
<li>
<p>Balances workload automatically</p></li>
<li>
<p>Prevents bottlenecks from overloaded individuals</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>How it works:</strong></p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p>&nbsp;&nbsp;Team Member A: 5 active tasks =E2=86=92 Skip</p></li>
<li>
<p>&nbsp;&nbsp;Team Member B: 2 active tasks =E2=86=92 Assign here</p></li>
<li>
<p>&nbsp;&nbsp;Team Member C: 7 active tasks =E2=86=92 Skip</p></li>
</ol></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong></p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p>&nbsp;Teams with varying work speeds</p></li>
<li>
<p>&nbsp;Preventing burnout</p></li>
<li>
<p>&nbsp;Optimizing throughput</p></li>
<li>
<p>&nbsp;When fairness matters more than rotation</p></li>
</ol></li>
</ol></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Round Robin</strong></p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p>Assigns in sequential rotation</p></li>
<li>
<p>Each team member gets next assignment in turn</p></li>
<li>
<p>Simple and predictable</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Example rotation:</strong></p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p>&nbsp;&nbsp;Assignment 1 =E2=86=92 User A</p></li>
<li>
<p>&nbsp;&nbsp;Assignment 2 =E2=86=92 User B</p></li>
<li>
<p>&nbsp;&nbsp;Assignment 3 =E2=86=92 User C</p></li>
<li>
<p>&nbsp;&nbsp;Assignment 4 =E2=86=92 User A (starts over)</p></li>
</ol></li>
</ol></li>
</ol>
<p></p>
<p><strong>Fortra Implementation Example:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>License Generation</strong>: Least Loaded (high volume, standard=
 process)</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Implementation Services</strong>: Context Based (route by produc=
t expertise)</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Training Scheduling</strong>: Round Robin (fair distribution amo=
ng trainers)</p></li>
</ul>
<p></p>
<h2 id=3D"OrderOrchestrationProcess-TaskExecutionTrigger"><strong>Task Exec=
ution Trigger</strong></h2>
<p>Determines the timing reference point for when a fulfillment step should=
 execute. This field is crucial for coordinating step execution with busine=
ss events:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Source Line Start Date</strong> - The step=E2=80=99s execution t=
iming is based on the start date of the order line item or contract line. T=
his ensures fulfillment activities align with when the customer=E2=80=99s s=
ervice or subscription period begins.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>For example, =E2=80=9CActivate Subscription=E2=80=9D would execute based=
 on the contract start date, ensuring services aren=E2=80=99t activated bef=
ore the customer=E2=80=99s term begins. This is ideal for subscription serv=
ices, maintenance contracts, or any fulfillment that should coincide with t=
he service period.</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Previous Steps=E2=80=99 Execute Date</strong> - The step=E2=80=
=99s execution timing is based on when the previous step in the fulfillment=
 flow was executed. This creates a sequential chain where each step follows=
 the completion of the prior step.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>For example, =E2=80=9CSend Welcome Email=E2=80=9D would execute immediat=
ely after =E2=80=9CCreate User Account=E2=80=9D completes. This is the most=
 common option for steps that have dependencies and must happen in a specif=
ic order, ensuring proper workflow sequencing.</p></li>
</ul></li>
</ul>
<p></p>
<p>The choice between these options depends on whether your fulfillment ste=
p is tied to a specific business date (like a contract start) or should sim=
ply follow the completion of previous activities in the fulfillment chain. =
This field works in conjunction with any wait times or delays configured on=
 the step to provide precise control over fulfillment timing.</p>
<p></p>
<h2 id=3D"OrderOrchestrationProcess-PointofNoReturn-ManagingFulfillmentReve=
rsibility"><strong>Point of No Return</strong> - Managing Fulfillment Rever=
sibility</h2>
<p>The Point of No Return field marks a critical threshold in your fulfillm=
ent process where actions become irreversible or would be costly/complex to=
 undo. This field helps manage fulfillment risk and ensures appropriate con=
trols are in place:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>True</strong> - Marks this step as a point where reversal become=
s difficult or impossible. Once a step marked as Point of No Return is comp=
leted, the system treats subsequent cancellations or modifications differen=
tly.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Examples include =E2=80=9CShip Physical Product,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9CProvi=
sion Perpetual License,=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9CExecute Third-Party Contract.=
=E2=80=9D These steps typically involve external systems, physical actions,=
 or legal commitments that cannot be easily reversed.</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p><strong>False</strong> - Indicates this step can be reversed or undone i=
f needed without significant impact. Most preparatory or internal steps fal=
l into this category, such as =E2=80=9CCreate Account Record,=E2=80=9D =E2=
=80=9CGenerate Quote Document,=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9CReserve Inventory.=E2=
=80=9D These actions can typically be rolled back without major business im=
plications.</p></li>
</ul>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
